tory goes  and yeah certainly in the story i  completely fall apart i mean in real  life  ironically i didn't fall apart because i  was writing the story i think that was  what was happening  but i mean with a bit more distance i'll  probably work out slightly more  what was going on what happens when the  lockdown ends i mean  are you concerned at all that the  creativity that that you you focused on  during this time you'll  you lose your muse almost  yeah yeah totally it was is a very kind  of  i mean once you embrace it and decide  that that's what you're going to do  that you decide to forget about the fact  that everyone will be writing about  lockdown once you decide yeah i'm going  to be one of those people  then it just it takes out an enormous  part of the writing process where  you're not trying to think of ideas and  you're not trying to  um you know come up with any spark of  you know originality  or start to write something it's just  all there and it's all around you so if  you decide to sort of swim  inside it all then yeah it's very useful  but i mean  i thought that at the end of the  lockdown and uh weirdly um there's  another lockdown  they keep on coming do you think there's  gonna be  that's not what i don't know so as i was  gonna say i don't want more and more  locked down so i can write more i would  like an end to it  but yeah there is a sort of silver  lining where i kind of think okay i'm  back in again i'll keep on writing  but are you worried that there'll be a  market for this book  because you know everyone's well most  people it seems have really hated  lockdown  does anybody want to be reminded of this  year you know is everyone gonna go  i mean i think lockdown itself is a  great time to enjoy poetry because you  might have a bit of extra time  to sit and go through books you haven't  had a chance to go through but do you  think in five years time we're going to  be saying  let's revisit that lockdown year  no i don't think so  so 